{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"query-collection-held-result-error-recency","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collection held-result error recency"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed PR head before this correction: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a4a8ba8d0c045dcad23f97d3b98dc7298281f1db"},{"type":"text","value":".\nReviewed semantic correction: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8bd3fc35"},{"type":"text","value":".\nPrimary owner: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/query-db-collection/tests/ownership-lifecycle.oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":" contract retains a completed failure\nuntil a later successful result applies. The added two-cell history starts with\none failed Query fetch, then holds a successful retry at a mocked persisted\nbaseline scan. A second refetch fails before the held success applies. At the\ncheckpoint after releasing the scan, the later failure must remain visible and\nthe count must be two. One cell uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clearError()"},{"type":"text","value":" and distinct Error objects;\nthe other uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch()"},{"type":"text","value":" and repeats the same Error object in one clock tick.\nThe expected state follows the order of completed failures and successful\napplications, without reading production error timestamps or revisions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the uncorrected PR head, both cells failed: the first observed\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError === undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" after the older application, and the second observed\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount === 1"},{"type":"text","value":" after two terminal failures. The correction guards error\nclearing with a revision captured when application begins and distinguishes\nterminal Query failures using Query Core's per-Query "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorUpdateCount"},{"type":"text","value":". It\nretains the older successful rows when they apply; it only prevents that\napplication from clearing an error recorded later."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The review's exact mutation-held sequence was also run on the uncorrected PR\nhead. It did not erase the newer error; that path published the earlier rows\nafter the handler settled while preserving "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":". The\npersisted path is the confirmed counterexample. The persisted scan is a\ncontrolled callback, not native SQLite execution. Native persistence,\nconcurrent retries for multiple tracked Queries, mutation-handler fetch\nsettlement, and deferred result application remain separate coverage cells in\nthe Query DB ownership row of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oracle-coverage.md"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":" comments and the owner state the law; the two histories and native-persistence limit are named above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Expected error identity and count come from the sequence of terminal failures and applications. The test does not use production timestamps, the revision guard, or Query Core's counter to compute expectations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The existing ownership oracle supplies its contract and model; the two-case history and real QueryClient driver sit beside the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":" refinement checks."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. This is a bounded deterministic two-cell history, not a generated property or a claim about all schedules."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The driver uses public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clearError"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refetch"},{"type":"text","value":", and Query Collection utils. It proves each fetch and scan occurred and checks public error identity and count both before and after releasing the scan."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Both cells failed at their intended public checkpoints on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a4a8ba8d"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8bd3fc35"},{"type":"text","value":". The first rejects premature clearing; the second rejects timestamp-and-object-only error deduplication."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No generated property or random campaign changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. No reference-model state was added, combined, or removed. The production revision has a separate error-event purpose."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Query fetch, result application, persisted baseline, and public error state use the glossary and owner terms; the test does not call a fetch completion a publication."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. No shrinking or normalized capture occurs. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":" block releases every controlled Promise; the owner cleanup collects failures without replacing the assertion failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The public identity/count observations distinguish the reported fault directly. The same-object cell challenges the proposed dedupe mechanism rather than supplying a second semantic reference model."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass for this bounded correction. This record names the reviewed semantic head, every applicable ORC-001–011 outcome, the killed wrong designs, and remaining coverage cells."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8bd3fc35"},{"type":"text","value":": 532/532 Query DB package tests passed across 15\nfiles; Query DB source typecheck passed; changed-file ESLint had zero errors\nand four warnings already present outside the edited lines; Prettier and Git\ndiff checks passed. The new persisted cases alone passed 2/2 on the PR branch."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production weight for this correction is 31 added and 17 removed lines, net\n+14 relative to the prior PR head. The complete PR production diff is 46\nadded and 24 removed lines, net +22 relative to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":". The added\nstate separates genuinely new terminal failures from observer re-notification\nand fences an older asynchronous application; it does not add retry or\nrecovery machinery. The oracle adds 91 lines, counted separately."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"query-collection-held-result-error-recency","text":"Query Collection held-result error recency","level":1}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Query Collection held-result error recency\n\nReviewed PR head before this correction: `a4a8ba8d0c045dcad23f97d3b98dc7298281f1db`.\nReviewed semantic correction: `8bd3fc35`.\nPrimary owner: `packages/query-db-collection/tests/ownership-lifecycle.oracle.test.ts`.\n\nThe public `lastError` and `errorCount` contract retains a completed failure\nuntil a later successful result applies. The added two-cell history starts with\none failed Query fetch, then holds a successful retry at a mocked persisted\nbaseline scan. A second refetch fails before the held success applies. At the\ncheckpoint after releasing the scan, the later failure must remain visible and\nthe count must be two. One cell uses `clearError()` and distinct Error objects;\nthe other uses `refetch()` and repeats the same Error object in one clock tick.\nThe expected state follows the order of completed failures and successful\napplications, without reading production error timestamps or revisions.\n\nOn the uncorrected PR head, both cells failed: the first observed\n`lastError === undefined` after the older application, and the second observed\n`errorCount === 1` after two terminal failures. The correction guards error\nclearing with a revision captured when application begins and distinguishes\nterminal Query failures using Query Core's per-Query `errorUpdateCount`. It\nretains the older successful rows when they apply; it only prevents that\napplication from clearing an error recorded later.\n\nThe review's exact mutation-held sequence was also run on the uncorrected PR\nhead. It did not erase the newer error; that path published the earlier rows\nafter the handler settled while preserving `lastError` and `errorCount`. The\npersisted path is the confirmed counterexample. The persisted scan is a\ncontrolled callback, not native SQLite execution. Native persistence,\nconcurrent retries for multiple tracked Queries, mutation-handler fetch\nsettlement, and deferred result application remain separate coverage cells in\nthe Query DB ownership row of `oracle-coverage.md`.\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Pass. The public `lastError`/`errorCount` comments and the owner state the law; the two histories and native-persistence limit are named above. |\n| ORC-002 | Pass. Expected error identity and count come from the sequence of terminal failures and applications. The test does not use production timestamps, the revision guard, or Query Core's counter to compute expectations. |\n| ORC-003 | Pass. The existing ownership oracle supplies its contract and model; the two-case history and real QueryClient driver sit beside the public `lastError`/`errorCount` refinement checks. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable. This is a bounded deterministic two-cell history, not a generated property or a claim about all schedules. |\n| ORC-005 | Pass. The driver uses public `loadSubset`, `clearError`/`refetch`, and Query Collection utils. It proves each fetch and scan occurred and checks public error identity and count both before and after releasing the scan. |\n| ORC-006 | Pass. Both cells failed at their intended public checkpoints on `a4a8ba8d` and pass at `8bd3fc35`. The first rejects premature clearing; the second rejects timestamp-and-object-only error deduplication. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable. No generated property or random campaign changed. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable. No reference-model state was added, combined, or removed. The production revision has a separate error-event purpose. |\n| ORC-009 | Pass. Query fetch, result application, persisted baseline, and public error state use the glossary and owner terms; the test does not call a fetch completion a publication. |\n| ORC-010 | Pass. No shrinking or normalized capture occurs. The `finally` block releases every controlled Promise; the owner cleanup collects failures without replacing the assertion failure. |\n| ORC-011 | Not applicable. The public identity/count observations distinguish the reported fault directly. The same-object cell challenges the proposed dedupe mechanism rather than supplying a second semantic reference model. |\n| ORC-012 | Pass for this bounded correction. This record names the reviewed semantic head, every applicable ORC-001–011 outcome, the killed wrong designs, and remaining coverage cells. |\n\nVerification at `8bd3fc35`: 532/532 Query DB package tests passed across 15\nfiles; Query DB source typecheck passed; changed-file ESLint had zero errors\nand four warnings already present outside the edited lines; Prettier and Git\ndiff checks passed. The new persisted cases alone passed 2/2 on the PR branch.\n\nProduction weight for this correction is 31 added and 17 removed lines, net\n+14 relative to the prior PR head. The complete PR production diff is 46\nadded and 24 removed lines, net +22 relative to `origin/main`. The added\nstate separates genuinely new terminal failures from observer re-notification\nand fences an older asynchronous application; it does not add retry or\nrecovery machinery. The oracle adds 91 lines, counted separately.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Query Collection held-result error recency\n\nReviewed PR head before this correction: `a4a8ba8d0c045dcad23f97d3b98dc7298281f1db`.\nReviewed semantic correction: `8bd3fc35`.\nPrimary owner: `packages/query-db-collection/tests/ownership-lifecycle.oracle.test.ts`.\n\nThe public `lastError` and `errorCount` contract retains a completed failure\nuntil a later successful result applies. The added two-cell history starts with\none failed Query fetch, then holds a successful retry at a mocked persisted\nbaseline scan. A second refetch fails before the held success applies. At the\ncheckpoint after releasing the scan, the later failure must remain visible and\nthe count must be two. One cell uses `clearError()` and distinct Error objects;\nthe other uses `refetch()` and repeats the same Error object in one clock tick.\nThe expected state follows the order of completed failures and successful\napplications, without reading production error timestamps or revisions.\n\nOn the uncorrected PR head, both cells failed: the first observed\n`lastError === undefined` after the older application, and the second observed\n`errorCount === 1` after two terminal failures. The correction guards error\nclearing with a revision captured when application begins and distinguishes\nterminal Query failures using Query Core's per-Query `errorUpdateCount`. It\nretains the older successful rows when they apply; it only prevents that\napplication from clearing an error recorded later.\n\nThe review's exact mutation-held sequence was also run on the uncorrected PR\nhead. It did not erase the newer error; that path published the earlier rows\nafter the handler settled while preserving `lastError` and `errorCount`. The\npersisted path is the confirmed counterexample. The persisted scan is a\ncontrolled callback, not native SQLite execution. Native persistence,\nconcurrent retries for multiple tracked Queries, mutation-handler fetch\nsettlement, and deferred result application remain separate coverage cells in\nthe Query DB ownership row of `oracle-coverage.md`.\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Pass. The public `lastError`/`errorCount` comments and the owner state the law; the two histories and native-persistence limit are named above. |\n| ORC-002 | Pass. Expected error identity and count come from the sequence of terminal failures and applications. The test does not use production timestamps, the revision guard, or Query Core's counter to compute expectations. |\n| ORC-003 | Pass. The existing ownership oracle supplies its contract and model; the two-case history and real QueryClient driver sit beside the public `lastError`/`errorCount` refinement checks. |\n| ORC-004 | Not applicable. This is a bounded deterministic two-cell history, not a generated property or a claim about all schedules. |\n| ORC-005 | Pass. The driver uses public `loadSubset`, `clearError`/`refetch`, and Query Collection utils. It proves each fetch and scan occurred and checks public error identity and count both before and after releasing the scan. |\n| ORC-006 | Pass. Both cells failed at their intended public checkpoints on `a4a8ba8d` and pass at `8bd3fc35`. The first rejects premature clearing; the second rejects timestamp-and-object-only error deduplication. |\n| ORC-007 | Not applicable. No generated property or random campaign changed. |\n| ORC-008 | Not applicable. No reference-model state was added, combined, or removed. The production revision has a separate error-event purpose. |\n| ORC-009 | Pass. Query fetch, result application, persisted baseline, and public error state use the glossary and owner terms; the test does not call a fetch completion a publication. |\n| ORC-010 | Pass. No shrinking or normalized capture occurs. The `finally` block releases every controlled Promise; the owner cleanup collects failures without replacing the assertion failure. |\n| ORC-011 | Not applicable. The public identity/count observations distinguish the reported fault directly. The same-object cell challenges the proposed dedupe mechanism rather than supplying a second semantic reference model. |\n| ORC-012 | Pass for this bounded correction. This record names the reviewed semantic head, every applicable ORC-001–011 outcome, the killed wrong designs, and remaining coverage cells. |\n\nVerification at `8bd3fc35`: 532/532 Query DB package tests passed across 15\nfiles; Query DB source typecheck passed; changed-file ESLint had zero errors\nand four warnings already present outside the edited lines; Prettier and Git\ndiff checks passed. The new persisted cases alone passed 2/2 on the PR branch.\n\nProduction weight for this correction is 31 added and 17 removed lines, net\n+14 relative to the prior PR head. The complete PR production diff is 46\nadded and 24 removed lines, net +22 relative to `origin/main`. The added\nstate separates genuinely new terminal failures from observer re-notification\nand fences an older asynchronous application; it does not add retry or\nrecovery machinery. The oracle adds 91 lines, counted separately.\n"}
